course: religion-ethics
lesson: 68
title: Missing someone: memory boxes, birthdays and special days
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Missing someone is what love does when the person is no longer there, and families find ways to keep that love close.
explain: Ellie and her mum make a memory box for Grandad: his glasses, a photo, the wrapper from his favourite sweets and a postcard, and she can open it whenever she misses him.
explain: Special days are often the hardest: on Grandad's birthday Ellie's family eat his favourite cake and tell stories about him, and at Christmas Lily's family set an empty chair for her mum.
explain: Grief can also jump out of nowhere: six months after Grandma died, Mia smells Grandma's perfume on a lady in a shop and starts crying; this is called a grief trigger, and it is normal.
explain: Christians light candles and pray for those they miss, many families plant a tree or keep a hoodie that still smells of Dad, and people of every faith and none keep photos, recordings and stories.
explain: Remembering on special days does not keep you stuck in sadness; it says «you are still part of us».
fact: A memory box is a special place to keep things that remind you of someone who has died.
fact: Many families mark a loved one's birthday by doing something that person loved, like eating their favourite cake.
fact: Holidays like Christmas can be very hard after a death, because they remind us who is missing.
fact: Some families set an empty chair or light a candle at a festival to remember the person.
fact: A grief trigger is a smell, song, place or object that suddenly brings back strong feelings of loss.
fact: Keeping clothes or things that smell like someone can bring comfort; many people do this.
fact: Anniversaries of a death can bring grief back strongly, even years later.
fact: Writing or talking about someone who has died keeps their memory alive.
fact: Many Christians and others plant trees or flowers in memory of loved ones.
fact: People who have died cannot read online posts; messages like «miss you» are for the living to share love.
fact: A recording of a loved one's voice can bring comfort and tears; it is okay to listen or not.
fact: Christians and people of other faiths remember the dead with prayers and customs on special days.
exercise: What is a memory box? | A box of things that remind you of someone | A special box for sending letters up to heaven | A box that must be buried with the person who died | A box for keeping old school reports and certificates in
exercise: Which things did Ellie put in Grandad's memory box? | His glasses, a photo, a sweet wrapper and a postcard | His car, his house keys and his bank card | Only new toys bought after the funeral | Flowers from the funeral, to keep for ever
exercise: Can Ellie keep her memory box for ever? | Yes, and look at it whenever she misses him | No, it must be thrown away after a year, when grief is over | No, only grown-ups are allowed to keep memory boxes like that | Yes, but she must never open it again once it is closed
exercise: Why do Ellie's family eat cake on Grandad's birthday? | To remember him and say he's still part of them | Because they have forgotten that he died | Because the law requires a cake every year after a death in the family | To celebrate that the sad time is over
exercise: Why can Christmas feel very hard after a death? | It reminds the family who is missing | Christmas is banned for a year after a death | Families aren't allowed presents after a funeral | It is always the coldest day of the year
exercise: Why did Lily's family set an empty chair at Christmas? | To remember her mum at the family table | Because they ran out of plates for the meal | Because Mum was coming back later that day | To make the table look more crowded
exercise: Why did Mia cry when she smelled a lady's perfume in the shop? | It was Grandma's perfume, a grief trigger | The perfume was too strong for her eyes | She was cross with the lady for wearing it | She was tired after a long day of shopping
exercise: What is a grief trigger? | A smell, song or place that brings back loss | A button inside you that turns sadness off completely, like a switch | A rule about how long you must be sad | A special prayer said at funerals only
exercise: Why does Jack sleep in his dad's old hoodie? | It still smells like his dad and comforts him | He has no other clothes to sleep in | His dad asked for it to be worn every night after he was gone | Hoodies keep ghosts away at night
exercise: Why might Zoe's mum go quiet on the anniversary of her brother's death? | Anniversaries can bring grief back strongly | She is angry with Zoe for something Zoe did at school | She has simply forgotten what day of the year it is | Grown-ups stop feeling any grief at all after a year
exercise: What can help a sad grown-up on an anniversary? | A gentle hug or a drawing | Loud jokes all day long | Asking her to cheer up now | Leaving her a list of jobs
exercise: Can Freddie write about his grandad, who died, for «My hero»? | Yes, it keeps his memory alive | No, heroes have to be alive | No, it would upset the teacher | Only if Grandad was famous
exercise: Why does Sophie plant a tree for her nan? | It is a living reminder that grows each year | Trees bring dead people back to life | The law says every death needs a tree | Nan's ashes must always go under trees
exercise: Can dead people read the «miss you» posts on social media? | No; the posts help the living share love | Yes, but only on the anniversary day | Yes, if the family remember to tag them in it, so it gets sent | No, because heaven has no Wi-Fi signal
exercise: Lily's dad keeps her mum's voice message on his phone. What is true? | It's a precious way to remember; listening is her choice | It is wrong to keep a dead person's message | Playing it will make her mum's ghost appear | It must be deleted at once so that the family can stop crying and heal
exercise: Which is a way Christians remember the dead on special days? | Lighting candles and praying for them | Never saying the person's name again | Throwing away all of their photos | Moving house so as to forget them
test: «Grandad's birthday cake when he's dead?? that's mad weird fam,» says Ellie's cousin from London (fam = mate). Fair answer? | Not weird; it's a way of saying he's still part of us | He's right; you should forget birthdays after a death | It's a spell to bring Grandad back to life | Only royal families may celebrate the dead
test: A rough older brother says, «Chuck that tatty hoodie in the bin, it's minging» (minging = smelly). Why might Jack want to keep it? | It smells of his dad and comforts him | It is the only warm hoodie in the house | His dad's ghost lives inside it | It will be worth a lot of money one day
test: What would happen if Mia smelled Grandma's perfume again next year? | It might bring sudden tears again, and that's normal | She would definitely feel nothing at all next time, because grief ends | It would mean her grief was never real | She would have to see a doctor at once
test: In a film, a girl buries all her mum's photos so she «won't be sad». What's a better idea from real life? | Keep memories close; they can comfort | Burying photos like that stops grief for ever | Photos only make grief last much, much longer | Forgetting completely is the only way to heal
test: «Can I keep it forever? Like FOREVER forever?» asks Ellie about her memory box. Best answer? | Yes, and open it whenever she misses him | No, memory boxes are only for a year | Only if she never shows it to anybody | No, it has to be buried with Grandad at the cemetery, next to him
test: A Gen Alpha kid says, «Posting 'miss u' to a dead uncle is so Ohio, he can't even see it» (so Ohio = weird). What's true? | He can't see it; the posts help the family share love | He can see it if enough people like the post | Posting about the dead is always disrespectful | The post goes straight to heaven if it's set to public, not private
test: The vicar announces, very formally: «On All Souls' Day we shall read aloud the names of those departed this year.» Why? | To remember them before God on a special day | To check that nobody is missing from church | To decide who goes to heaven this year | To punish families who forgot to come
test: «Me mam's dead quiet today, she won't talk, what've I done?» asks Zoe in Newcastle. It's the anniversary of her brother's death. Best answer? | Nothing; Mum is remembering her son and feeling sad | Zoe must have done something very naughty that Mum found out about | Mum has stopped loving the family | Mum has forgotten what day it is
test: «I'm gonna plant a tree for my nan, will it make me remember her?» asks Sophie. Best answer? | It can be a living reminder that grows each year | Trees make you forget so it hurts less | Only oak trees work for remembering someone who has died | It only works if Nan's ashes are under it
test: A boy in Glasgow says, «Ah cannae listen tae Mam's voice message, it does ma heid in» (cannae = can't, heid = head). What's right? | It's okay not to listen; it's his choice | He must listen to it every day to stay close | He should delete it at once, to stop the pain | Not listening means he didn't really love her
test: Myth to check: «Remembering someone on special days keeps you stuck in sadness.» | False; it is a way of keeping love close | True; you should forget special days | True; it stops grief ever getting softer | False, but only after ten years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a memory box? | It preserves objects associated with a deceased person so that they may be remembered | It is a receptacle in which correspondence is dispatched to the deceased | It is required to be interred together with the deceased | It is intended to prevent the bereaved from remembering the deceased
exam: Identify the term denoting a sensation, such as a scent, which suddenly occasions renewed feelings of loss. | A grief trigger | A memorial service | An anniversary | A memory box
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning messages addressed to the deceased upon social media. | They are not read by the deceased but enable the living to share their affection | They are received by the deceased upon the anniversary of death | They are prohibited by the principal religious traditions | They cause the deceased to appear in dreams
exam: Whereas festivals and anniversaries frequently renew grief, ascertain which practice is commonly observed by bereaved families. | The lighting of candles, prayers, or the setting of an empty place in remembrance | The prohibition of all mention of the deceased | The removal of every photograph from the household | The cancellation of all festivals for a period of ten years
